WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.519
When you're deploying an MCP server to production, development assumptions break fast.

2
00:00:05.519 --> 00:00:08.760
Development servers trust the local process.

3
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.520
Production servers need to answer harder questions.

4
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.159
Who's calling? Are they authorized?

5
00:00:15.159 --> 00:00:17.600
How do you handle multiple client versions?

6
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.600
And what happens under load?

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.200
In this lesson, we'll work through authentication, versioning, rate limiting, and monitoring.

8
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.200
Let's get into it.

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.840
A production MCP server has to answer for itself in ways a local one never does.

10
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:39.520
MCP authentication is the mechanism by which a client proves its identity to a remote MCP

11
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.799
server before accessing tools and resources.

12
00:00:42.799 --> 00:00:49.159
For remote streamable HTTP servers, MCP defines an optional OAuth 2.1-based authorization

13
00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:52.200
flow as the recommended mechanism.

14
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.360
Simpler options like API keys still work, but OAuth is the standardized path the spec

15
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.759
points you toward.

16
00:00:58.759 --> 00:01:04.279
Semantic versioning uses major, minor, and patch numbers to signal how much a change

17
00:01:04.279 --> 00:01:06.160
breaks existing consumers.

18
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.800
A major bump means breaking changes.

19
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.080
Minor means new features that are backward compatible.

20
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.879
Patch means bug fixes.

21
00:01:13.879 --> 00:01:20.360
And rate limiting is capping how many requests a client or user can send per unit time, protecting

22
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:27.160
shared infrastructure from overload, and preventing one client from crowding out all the others.

23
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:33.639
A local MCP server running on a developer's machine can trust the process that called it.

24
00:01:33.639 --> 00:01:35.720
There's no network boundary to cross.

25
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.160
A remote production server is completely different.

26
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.440
Any client that can reach the endpoint over the network could try to invoke its tools.

27
00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.879
Without authentication, there's no way to know who is calling.

28
00:01:47.879 --> 00:01:53.839
There's no stable identity to attach to audit logs, and no foundation for authorization decisions.

29
00:01:53.839 --> 00:01:59.720
You can't limit which tools a client can invoke if you don't know who the client is.

30
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.519
Authentication is the prerequisite for everything else in a secure production deployment.

31
00:02:04.519 --> 00:02:09.240
It enables authorization, meaningful logging, and per-client rate limiting.

32
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:16.440
A production MCP server without authentication is essentially a public API for every tool it exposes.

33
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.440
MCP's specification defines an optional OAuth 2.1 authorization flow as the recommended

34
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.720
mechanism for remote streamable HTTP servers.

35
00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.720
The flow works as you'd expect.

36
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.839
The client goes through an OAuth authorization flow with an identity provider to obtain an access token.

37
00:02:35.839 --> 00:02:39.720
That token is included with each request to the MCP server.

38
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.639
The server validates the token and reads the scopes it carries.

39
00:02:43.839 --> 00:02:49.520
Crucially, those scopes can map directly to which tools the client is allowed to invoke.

40
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.199
A token with a read-only scope should only be able to call read tools.

41
00:02:54.199 --> 00:02:57.440
This design keeps the MCP server stateless.

42
00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.600
It doesn't manage sessions or store credentials itself.

43
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:07.720
The identity provider handles that complexity, and the MCP server just validates tokens on every request.

44
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:13.880
OAuth 2.1 is the answer to reach for when the question involves authenticating clients

45
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:19.880
to a remote streamable HTTP MCP server, but it's useful to understand the alternatives

46
00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.399
and why they fall short for this use case.

47
00:03:22.399 --> 00:03:26.880
API keys are simple shared secrets passed in request headers.

48
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.679
They're easy to implement but provide no per-user identity, no scope granularity, and no standard

49
00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:34.160
revocation flow.

50
00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.559
A shared key can't tell you which user made a call.

51
00:03:37.559 --> 00:03:44.279
Mutual TLS, or MTLS, authenticates both client and server using certificates.

52
00:03:44.279 --> 00:03:49.080
It's strong for service-to-service scenarios, but adds certificate management overhead that

53
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.720
most MCP deployments don't need.

54
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:57.479
OAuth handles the human-in-the-loop case where an actual user needs to authorize a client

55
00:03:57.479 --> 00:04:00.479
to access tools on their behalf.

56
00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.880
Before we leave authentication, there's a set of transport-level security rules the

57
00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:12.600
MCP specification is unusually firm about for streamable HTTP servers, and they're easy to miss.

58
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:18.440
First, the server is required to validate the origin header on every incoming connection.

59
00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.760
This is what stops a DNS rebinding attack, where a malicious web page tricks a browser

60
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.760
into talking to your server.

61
00:04:25.799 --> 00:04:34.519
Second, when the server runs locally, it should bind only to the loopback address, 127.0.0.1,

62
00:04:34.519 --> 00:04:37.320
rather than to all network interfaces.

63
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.959
Binding to everything exposes a local server to the whole network.

64
00:04:41.959 --> 00:04:45.600
Third, the server should authenticate its connections.

65
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.679
Notice the strength here.

66
00:04:47.679 --> 00:04:50.079
Validating origin is mandatory in the spec.

67
00:04:50.079 --> 00:04:52.640
The other two are strong recommendations.

68
00:04:52.640 --> 00:05:04.119
Versioning MCP servers is about managing change without breaking clients that depend on tools

69
00:05:04.119 --> 00:05:05.839
that already exist.

70
00:05:05.839 --> 00:05:07.720
The rule is straightforward.

71
00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:12.040
Backward-compatible changes don't require a version bump that breaks clients.

72
00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:16.880
Adding a new optional tool, adding optional fields to an existing schema, or fixing a

73
00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:23.200
bug in a handler while clients that haven't updated continue working exactly as before.

74
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.119
Breaking changes are different.

75
00:05:25.119 --> 00:05:30.119
Removing a tool, renaming it, or changing required argument names or types will break

76
00:05:30.119 --> 00:05:32.839
any client that calls that tool.

77
00:05:32.839 --> 00:05:37.799
These need a major version bump, a migration window, where both old and new versions are

78
00:05:37.799 --> 00:05:44.600
served simultaneously, and explicit deprecation notices before the old version is retired.

79
00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.480
The pattern is the same as any other API.

80
00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.640
Communicate changes early and give clients time to adapt.

81
00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:56.799
Rate limiting is how a shared MCP server stays healthy when multiple clients are hitting

82
00:05:56.799 --> 00:05:58.600
it simultaneously.

83
00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:03.160
Without limits, a single client running a heavy batch job can saturate the server's

84
00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:07.200
resources and make it unresponsive for everyone else.

85
00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.880
The baseline practice is a per-client cap, on requests per time window.

86
00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:15.760
Beyond that, expensive operations need their own tighter limits.

87
00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:22.059
A tool that makes external API calls or runs database queries has a different cost profile

88
00:06:22.059 --> 00:06:24.399
than a tool that does a simple lookup.

89
00:06:24.399 --> 00:06:30.640
When a client hits a limit, the server should return an HTTP 429 response with a retry after

90
00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:34.239
header, so the client knows when it can try again.

91
00:06:34.239 --> 00:06:40.040
Rate limits make infrastructure costs predictable and protect all clients equally.

92
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:47.160
Monitoring a production MCP server means treating it like any other API service in your stack.

93
00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:52.119
Every tool invocation should generate a log entry that captures the caller's identity,

94
00:06:52.119 --> 00:06:57.920
a sanitized summary of the arguments, the result status, and the end-to-end latency.

95
00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:03.040
With that data, you can track error rates by tool and detect when a downstream dependency

96
00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:04.640
starts degrading.

97
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:10.160
You can set alerts when error rates or P99 latency breach thresholds you define.

98
00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:15.160
You can review access patterns to spot unexpected usage or potential misuse.

99
00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:20.519
Visibility into which tools are being called, by whom, and at what frequency is also directly

100
00:07:20.519 --> 00:07:22.880
useful for capacity planning.

101
00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:27.799
Without this observability, you're operating a production service blind.

102
00:07:27.799 --> 00:07:32.160
Three reliability practices every production MCP server needs.

103
00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:38.720
First, a dedicated health endpoint that load balancers and uptime monitors can pull without

104
00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.559
invoking real tools.

105
00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:45.480
This lets infrastructure verify the server is responsive without creating side effects

106
00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:47.279
in the tools themselves.

107
00:07:47.279 --> 00:07:53.140
Second, graceful degradation when a downstream dependency is unavailable.

108
00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:58.239
Return a structured error result instead of crashing or hanging, so clients can retry

109
00:07:58.239 --> 00:08:00.600
or fall back without a hard failure.

110
00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:07.480
Third, hard timeouts on tool handler execution, so a slow downstream system can't hold a connection

111
00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:11.640
open indefinitely and exhaust the server's connection pool.

112
00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:16.600
These three practices cover the most common production failure modes, health checks, graceful

113
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:19.920
errors, and timeouts.

114
00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.239
An MCP server in production is talking to callers it can't see.

115
00:08:24.239 --> 00:08:27.600
Locally, it just trusts the process that started it.

116
00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:33.119
Remotely, it has no idea who's asking or how many of them there are.

117
00:08:33.119 --> 00:08:38.599
That matters because every assumption you skipped in development shows up later as an incident.

118
00:08:38.599 --> 00:08:44.359
One thing to watch is the slow dependency, because it takes the whole server down without

119
00:08:44.359 --> 00:08:46.919
ever looking like an attack.

120
00:08:46.919 --> 00:08:48.200
That's it for this one.

121
00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.440
I'll see you in the next video.

